# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Jonah'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'prophets'
SUBTITLE = 'The Prophet’s Flight, Nineveh’s Repentance, and the Mercy of God'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Jonah opens with “the word of the LORD” coming to “Jonah the son of Amittai,” who is told to arise, go to Nineveh and cry against it (Jonah 1:1–2). The book follows his flight toward the sea, the great fish prepared by the LORD, his prayer out of the fish’s belly, the preaching in Nineveh, the city’s turning, and the LORD’s closing question to a prophet who was displeased (Jonah 1:17; 2:1; 3:4–10; 4:1, 11).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'Jonah is the book of a prophet of the LORD sent from Israel’s side of the sea to a Gentile city, and its place is Israel’s Prophecy Program: Jonah speaks of himself as a Hebrew and looks toward the LORD’s holy temple (Jonah 1:9; 2:4). Its words on mercy and repentance are also placed in the Universal Truths Table.')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Jonah', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — The Flight (Ch. 1)'), ('p', 'The word of the LORD sends Jonah to Nineveh, Jonah goes down toward the sea, the LORD sends out a great wind, and the lot falls upon Jonah (Jonah 1:1–4, 7, 15).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Prayer from the Fish (Ch. 2)'), ('p', 'The LORD prepares a great fish, and Jonah prays to the LORD his God out of the fish’s belly (Jonah 1:17; 2:1, 4, 6).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — Nineveh Hears (Ch. 3)'), ('p', 'The word of the LORD comes the second time, Jonah cries that Nineveh shall be overthrown, and the people believe God and turn from their evil way. The book reports what they are said to have believed and what followed, as written (Jonah 3:1–5, 8–10).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — The Question (Ch. 4)'), ('p', 'Jonah is displeased, the LORD God prepares a gourd and a worm and a vehement east wind, and the book ends with the LORD’s question about sparing Nineveh (Jonah 4:1, 6–9, 11).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Jonah', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Word of the LORD Comes</b> — The book begins with the word of the LORD coming to a named prophet, not with the prophet’s own plan (Jonah 1:1; 3:1).', '<b>The LORD Prepares</b> — The LORD sends the wind, prepares the fish, and prepares the gourd, the worm and the east wind (Jonah 1:4, 17; 4:6–8).', '<b>Nineveh Believed God</b> — “So the people of Nineveh believed God” is reported with the fast, the sackcloth and the turning from the evil way (Jonah 3:5, 8).', '<b>God Repented of the Evil</b> — “God saw their works, that they turned from their evil way; and God repented of the evil” (Jonah 3:10).', '<b>A Question Left Standing</b> — “Should not I spare Nineveh, that great city” is the last word of the book, and Jonah’s answer is not recorded (Jonah 4:11).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Jonah', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Jonah points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Prophet Who Went Down and Was Brought Up</b> — Jonah’s descent into the sea and his life brought up from corruption are reported as written in his prayer (Jonah 2:5–6).', '<b>The Sign of Jonah</b> — The book does not itself mention a sign; the Lord’s later words about Jonah are read in their own chapters, not here.'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Jonah', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Jonah speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Jonah operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Jonah in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Jonah reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['the word of the LORD to a named prophet of Israel, Jonah the son of Amittai (Jonah 1:1)', 'a prophet who calls himself a Hebrew (Jonah 1:9)', 'the LORD’s holy temple as the place toward which Jonah looks (Jonah 2:4)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Jonah that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Jonah contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['The Word to a Prophet — “Now the word of the LORD came unto Jonah the son of Amittai.” (Jonah 1:1).', 'Sent to Nineveh — “Arise, go to Nineveh, that great city, and cry against it.” (Jonah 1:2).', 'Toward the Holy Temple — “yet I will look again toward thy holy temple.” (Jonah 2:4).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Jonah Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Jonah and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Jonah teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Jonah', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['The Word to a Prophet', '“Now the word of the LORD came unto Jonah the son of Amittai.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Jonah 1:1'], ['Sent to Nineveh', '“Arise, go to Nineveh, that great city, and cry against it.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Jonah 1:2'], ['Toward the Holy Temple', '“yet I will look again toward thy holy temple.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Jonah 2:4']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Jonah that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Jonah teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['Honest Confession', '“for I know that for my sake this great tempest is upon you.”', 'Jonah 1:12'], ['Called to Pray', '“arise, call upon thy God.”', 'Jonah 1:6'], ['Forsaking Mercy', '“They that observe lying vanities forsake their own mercy.”', 'Jonah 2:8'], ['Turning from Evil', '“let them turn every one from his evil way, and from the violence that is in their hands.”', 'Jonah 3:8'], ['God Repented of the Evil', '“God saw their works, that they turned from their evil way; and God repented of the evil.”', 'Jonah 3:10'], ['Sparing a City', '“should not I spare Nineveh, that great city.”', 'Jonah 4:11']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Jonah without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Jonah in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Confess honestly</b> — Own the cause of the trouble (Jonah 1:12).', '<b>Call upon God</b> — Arise, call upon thy God (Jonah 1:6).', '<b>Turn from evil</b> — From the evil way and the violence in the hands (Jonah 3:8).', '<b>Hold to mercy</b> — Do not forsake it for lying vanities (Jonah 2:8).', '<b>Weigh your anger</b> — Hear the LORD’s question about the city (Jonah 4:11).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Jonah', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Jonah in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['The Flight (Ch. 1) — The word of the LORD and the lot. <i>(Jonah 1:1–2, 6, 12, 15)</i>', 'The Prayer from the Fish (Ch. 2) — Toward the holy temple. <i>(Jonah 2:1, 4, 6, 8)</i>', 'Nineveh Hears (Ch. 3) — They believed God and turned. <i>(Jonah 3:1–2, 5, 8, 10)</i>', 'The Question (Ch. 4) — Should not I spare. <i>(Jonah 4:1, 6–9, 11)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Jonah, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward mercy, repentance, and compassion toward others without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Jonah simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Jonah stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
